  "query_preview": "..Today...A low pressure system currently spinning \noff the coast of northern Baja California is visible in mid- \nlevel water vapor satellite imagery. This is expected to slowly \ndrift northeastward while weakening through Sunday-Monday. A \nsoutherly moisture surge is currently underway, somewhat enhanced \nby continued active tropical cyclone activity in the Eastern \nPacific. Combined with synoptic lift from the approaching weather \nsystem, we are seeing bands of light showers across Northern \nArizona early this morning. Observed rainfall has remained very \nlight thus far due to lingering dry surface conditions, though \nsurface dewpoints are quickly rising. Moisture will continue to \nstream into Arizona through the day today, with PWAT rising well \nabove normal for mid October. However cloud cover will limit \ndaytime heating, and weak flow will limit orographic influences. \nAs a result, total rainfall accumulations through Sunday evening \nare expected to remain light, up to around 0.2-0.4 inches. Besides\nshowers, expect continued cool daytime temperatures and light \nwinds.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...A strong low pressure system will rapidly dive \nalong the west coast and approach Arizona. This will reinforce a \npressure gradient bringing south-southwesterly flow to our region. \nWe will see gradually strengthening southerly surface winds \nthrough the period. Lingering moisture will also lead to continued\nchances for daytime showers and thunderstorms, especially Monday \nafternoon. Daytime temperatures will climb a few degrees, but \nstill stay near to below October normals.\n\nWednesday through Friday...The strong low pressure system will \nbegin to push inland and carry a deeper surge of moisture to \nnorthern AZ. Widespread showers and thunderstorms, some strong, \nare expected on Wednesday and Thursday. The current outlook brings\nthe best moisture and rainfall accumulations across eastern AZ. \nStronger low-level flow with orographic influences is also \nexpected. Most areas will see at least 0.25-0.75 inches of rain, \nwith preferred upslope regions across eastern AZ seeing as much as\n1.5-2.5 inches. Ensemble guidance still does have some spread in \nthe trajectory and how progressive the trough will be, so there is\nroom for change in total accumulations and where the deepest \nmoisture will land. Activity is expected to trend downward by \nFriday into the weekend. Also expect even cooler temperatures with\ngusty south to southwest winds each day, strongest on Thursday.",
